## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical CISSP alignment on secure messaging design: threat modeling, cryptography, device attestation, revocation, and incident response, across multiple platforms.

## Key Concepts
- End-to-end encryption and forward secrecy
- Device attestation and binding keys to a trusted device
- Key rotation, revocation, and audit logging
- Threat modeling methods (STRIDE/PASTA)

## Code Example
```javascript
// Example: rotate per-user keys (simplified)
async function rotateUserKey(userId) {
  const newKey = await crypto.subtle.generateKey({name:'AES-GCM', length:256}, true, ['encrypt','decrypt']);
  await storeKey(userId, newKey);
  await revokeOldKeys(userId);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you validate key compromise containment in a live system?
- What logs and metrics would you collect to support forensics without exposing plaintext data?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical CISSP application to real-world, multi-region auth flows, highlighting threat modeling, token lifecycle, and IR readiness.

## Key Concepts
- STRIDE/PASTA for auth/session threats
- JWT, PKCE, JWKS, token rotation, revocation
- mTLS, RBAC/ABAC in a multi-region service mesh
- IR playbooks and testing strategy

## Code Example
```javascript
// Token validation sketch
function validateToken(token, jwks) {
  const payload = decode(token);
  if (payload.iss !== expectedIssuer) return false;
  if (payload.exp < Date.now()/1000) return false;
  // signature verify against jwks...
  return true;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you validate revocation in a scalable way?
- What metrics indicate token compromise?
